ody paragraphs, and a visionary conclusion synthesizing the central philosophical thesis."}, "point_by_point_audit": [{"page": 1, "what_you_wrote": "...", "evaluator_remark": "The page is completely blank. No attempt was made to write on the essay topic.", "verdict": "Superficial", "badge": "Philosophical Hook"}], "visual_annotations": [{"page": 1, "box_2d": [100, 100, 900, 900], "label": "Unattempted Booklet", "text": "Entire page is blank. Zero marks awarded.", "type": "critical"}], "rubric_scores": {"intro_score": 0.0, "core_demand_score": 0.0, "value_add_score": 0.0, "presentation_score": 0.0, "context_score": 0.0}, "upsc_exam_hall_discipline": {"handwriting_legibility": "Clean & Readable", "margin_discipline": "Maintained strictly", "word_count_compliance": "Target: ~1000-1200 words across booklet pages (0 words written)"}, "next_attempt_focus": {"thesis_crystallization": "Begin with a compelling Socratic or philosophical inquiry defining courage not as fearlessness, but as the active governance of fear by reason and moral duty.", "student_draft_quote": "...", "booklet_placement": "Page 1, Introduction Paragraph 1", "examiner_upgrade_sentence": "True courage is not the stony apathy of the fearless, but the vibrating tension of a soul fully aware of peril yet choosing duty over despair."}, "model_answer": "Blueprint for ''Courage is resistance to fear, mastery of fear, not absence of fear'':\n1. Introduction: Define courage through Mark Twain''s perspective (''Courage is resistance to fear, mastery of fear, not absence of fear''). Contrast the foolhardy absence of fear with true moral and physical courage.\n2. Philosophical & Ethical Dimension: Mark Twain, Aristotle''s Golden Mean (courage as the mean between cowardice and rashness), Bhagavad Gita (Arjuna overcoming paralysis of fear through duty/Dharma).\n3. Historical & Freedom Struggle Dimension: Indian freedom fighters facing colonial repression; Nelson Mandela defining courage not as the absence of fear, but the triumph over it.\n4. Socio-Cultural & Psychological Dimension: Overcoming social stigmas, whistleblowers, marginalized communities fighting systemic oppression, mental health resilience.\n5. Administrative & Governance Dimension: Civil servants taking bold policy decisions under political or bureaucratic pressure, standing up for integrity.\n6. Scientific & Technological Dimension: Scientists venturing into the unknown (space exploration, pandemic vaccine development like Covaxin/Covishield despite uncertainty).\n7. Conclusion: Synthesize that fear is a natural biological sentinel, but courage is the human capacity to subordinate fear to higher ethical ideals, ensuring human progress and justice.", "transcribed_text": "[Page 1]\n", "_meta_eval_id": "eval_1792237107_48cc05", "_meta_created_at": "2026-10-17 11:38:27", "created_at": "2026-10-17 11:38:27", "evaluated_at": "2026-10-17 11:38:27", "_meta_user_email": "guest@cookedmains.in", "_meta_paper": "Essay", "_meta_max_marks": 125, "_meta_is_rewrite": 0, "_meta_baseline_eval_id": null, "_meta_file_hash": null, "_meta_visual_hashes": ["0000000000000000"], "_meta_thumbnail": "data:image/jpeg;base64,", "_meta_pages": ["data:image/jpeg;base64,"]}', '["data:image/jpeg;base64,"]', 'data:image/jpeg;base64,', 0, '', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-10-17 11:38:27')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

