for essay writing including a standard introduction, 4-5 thematic body paragraphs, and a forward-looking conclusion."}, "point_by_point_audit": [{"page": 1, "what_you_wrote": "", "evaluator_remark": "The page is completely blank. No substantive argument or evidence was presented.", "verdict": "Superficial", "badge": "Philosophical Hook"}], "visual_annotations": [{"page": 1, "box_2d": [100, 100, 900, 900], "label": "Unattempted Question", "text": "Zero marks awarded. Entire answer booklet left blank.", "type": "critical"}], "rubric_scores": {"intro_score": 0.0, "core_demand_score": 0.0, "value_add_score": 0.0, "presentation_score": 0.0, "context_score": 0.0}, "upsc_exam_hall_discipline": {"handwriting_legibility": "Clean & Readable", "margin_discipline": "Maintained strictly", "word_count_compliance": "0 words (Target: 1000-1200 words)"}, "next_attempt_focus": {"thesis_crystallization": "Must define the core philosophical premise in the opening 150 words rather than leaving the booklet blank.", "student_draft_quote": "[No text available]", "booklet_placement": "Page 1, Paragraph 1", "examiner_upgrade_sentence": "Courage is not the absence of fear, but the triumph over it. The brave man is not he who does not feel afraid, but he who conquers that fear."}, "model_answer": "Introduction: Begin with Mark Twain''s insight: ''Courage is resistance to fear, mastery of fear, not absence of fear.'' Define fear as a natural evolutionary instinct and courage as a conscious ethical choice. Body Paragraph 1 (Philosophical & Ethical): Contrast recklessness (absence of fear) with true courage (mastery of fear). Aristotle''s doctrine of the mean where courage lies between cowardice and rashness. Body Paragraph 2 (Historical & Freedom Struggle): Mahatma Gandhi''s philosophy of Satyagraha\u2014overcoming the fear of the oppressor through moral force. Nelson Mandela''s observation that courage is the triumph over fear. Body Paragraph 3 (Socio-Cultural & Gender): Overcoming societal dogmas, patriarchal norms, and institutional barriers. Women standing up against regressive traditions. Body Paragraph 4 (Administrative & Civil Services): The courage of conviction for a civil servant\u2014standing up against political expediency, upholding constitutional morality, and speaking truth to power. Body Paragraph 5 (Global & Scientific): Scientific endeavors, space exploration, and handling global crises like pandemics where uncertainty breeds fear. Conclusion: Synthesize that in an increasingly complex world, true progress relies not on fearless individuals, but on those who acknowledge fear and act with moral and physical resilience.", "transcribed_text": "[Page 1]\n", "_meta_eval_id": "eval_1792237058_d2a573", "_meta_created_at": "2026-10-17 11:37:38", "created_at": "2026-10-17 11:37:38", "evaluated_at": "2026-10-17 11:37:38", "_meta_user_email": "guest@cookedmains.in", "_meta_paper": "Essay", "_meta_max_marks": 125, "_meta_is_rewrite": 0, "_meta_baseline_eval_id": null, "_meta_file_hash": null, "_meta_visual_hashes": ["0000000000000000"], "_meta_thumbnail": "data:image/jpeg;base64,", "_meta_pages": ["data:image/jpeg;base64,"]}', '["data:image/jpeg;base64,"]', 'data:image/jpeg;base64,', 0, '', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-10-17 11:37:38')
