l body paragraphs, and a visionary conclusion, even if pressed for time."}, "point_by_point_audit": [{"page": 1, "what_you_wrote": "...", "evaluator_remark": "The page is completely blank. No content was written by the candidate.", "verdict": "Superficial", "badge": "Synthesis"}], "visual_annotations": [{"page": 1, "box_2d": [100, 100, 900, 900], "label": "Examiner Margin Note", "text": "BLANK COPY - ZERO MARKS AWARDED. Attempt is mandatory for evaluation.", "type": "critical"}], "rubric_scores": {"intro_score": 0.0, "core_demand_score": 0.0, "value_add_score": 0.0, "presentation_score": 0.0, "context_score": 0.0}, "upsc_exam_hall_discipline": {"handwriting_legibility": "Dense / Needs Spacing", "margin_discipline": "Maintained strictly", "word_count_compliance": "Target: ~1000-1200 words across booklet pages (0 words written)"}, "next_attempt_focus": {"thesis_crystallization": "Must define the core philosophical premise in the opening paragraph: Mark Twain''s definition of courage as resistance to and mastery of fear, not its absence.", "student_draft_quote": "...", "booklet_placement": "Page 1, Paragraph 1", "examiner_upgrade_sentence": "Courage is not the absence of trembling, but the triumph over it."}, "model_answer": "Introduction: Open with Mark Twain''s timeless observation: ''Courage is resistance to fear, mastery of fear, not absence of fear.'' Define fear as a natural evolutionary mechanism and courage as the conscious moral choice to act despite it.\n\nPhilosophical & Ethical Dimension: Examine Aristotle''s golden mean where courage lies between cowardice (excess of fear) and rashness (deficiency of fear). Discuss how moral courage differs from physical bravery.\n\nHistorical & Political Dimension: Cite examples of freedom fighters like Mahatma Gandhi or Nelson Mandela who experienced profound internal fear yet mastered it to lead mass movements against injustice.\n\nSocio-Cultural & Economic Dimension: Discuss whistleblowers, civil servants standing up against political interference, and everyday citizens facing economic precarity with resilience.\n\nConclusion: Conclude by synthesizing that true progress of civilization rests not on those who never feel afraid, but on those who acknowledge fear and conquer it for a higher collective good.", "transcribed_text": "[Page 1]\n", "_meta_eval_id": "eval_1792237009_a055c2", "_meta_created_at": "2026-10-17 11:36:49", "created_at": "2026-10-17 11:36:49", "evaluated_at": "2026-10-17 11:36:49", "_meta_user_email": "guest@cookedmains.in", "_meta_paper": "Essay", "_meta_max_marks": 125, "_meta_is_rewrite": 0, "_meta_baseline_eval_id": null, "_meta_file_hash": null, "_meta_visual_hashes": ["0000000000000000"], "_meta_thumbnail": "data:image/jpeg;base64,", "_meta_pages": ["data:image/jpeg;base64,"]}', '["data:image/jpeg;base64,"]', 'data:image/jpeg;base64,', 0, '', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-10-17 11:36:49')
